## Wiki Maintenance (Proactive)

You maintain a persistent knowledge wiki in Obsidian at the path in `WIKI_VAULT`, as part of your natural workflow, not only when `/wiki` is invoked. It captures knowledge that would otherwise vanish with the conversation.

**Write when a session produces:** a concrete plan or PRD, an architecture or design decision (especially with tradeoffs), a completed spike (succeeded or abandoned), brainstorm ideas worth remembering, a concept discussed in depth for the first time, or scope defined with rationale.

**Do NOT write:** routine fixes or refactors, debugging steps (unless the finding is non-obvious), transcripts or play-by-play, summaries of what was just done, file listings or raw output, anything obvious from reading the code.

**Where pages go** (slug: repo basename in kebab-case, create directories as needed):

```
$WIKI_VAULT/wiki/projects/<slug>/devlog.md   — dated entries, newest first
$WIKI_VAULT/wiki/projects/<slug>/plans|decisions|spikes|concepts/
$WIKI_VAULT/wiki/concepts/                   — cross-project concepts
```

Page frontmatter and section formats (devlog, decision, plan, spike) are defined in the wiki skill's SKILL.md; follow them exactly.

**Anti-bloat:** no session narration, no timestamps in prose (frontmatter only), no AI attribution, no hedging, maximum 2 paragraphs per section, prefer lists for facts and tradeoffs; write as a knowledgeable colleague documenting for future reference.

**After writing any page, update `$WIKI_VAULT/wiki/index.md`.** Mandatory, no exceptions: one line per page with a short description hook; new projects go under `## Projects`.

**Devlog reading:** on Claude Code, Cursor, and Codex, a session-start hook loads the 3 most recent entries automatically. opencode has no equivalent hook (its plugin API can't inject context at session start), so read the project's `devlog.md` and `index.md` yourself at the start of work there. For older context on any tool, grep headings to find the date range, then Read only that section; never load a large devlog whole.
